## 6. CI/CD Pipeline (GitHub Actions)

### Recommended: GitHub Actions

**Why:** Your repo is already on GitHub. GitHub Actions is free for public repos and has 2000 min/month for private repos.

### Backend Pipeline — `.github/workflows/backend-deploy.yml`

```yaml
name: Deploy Backend to Azure App Service

on:
  push:
    branches: [sachinthana/hybrid]
    paths: ['backend/**']
  workflow_dispatch:

jobs:
  deploy:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Set up Python
        uses: actions/setup-python@v5
        with:
          python-version: '3.10'

      - name: Install dependencies
        working-directory: ./backend
        run: |
          python -m pip install --upgrade pip
          pip install -r requirements.txt

      - name: Zip backend
        working-directory: ./backend
        run: |
          zip -r ../backend.zip . -x "__pycache__/*" "*.pyc" ".env" "temp/*"

      - name: Deploy to Azure App Service
        uses: azure/webapps-deploy@v3
        with:
          app-name: app-sinhala-backend
          publish-profile: ${{ secrets.AZURE_BACKEND_PUBLISH_PROFILE }}
          package: backend.zip
```

### Frontend Pipeline — Auto-configured by Static Web Apps

Azure Static Web Apps automatically creates a GitHub Actions workflow when you link your repo. No manual setup needed.

### Secrets to Configure in GitHub:
- `AZURE_BACKEND_PUBLISH_PROFILE` — Download from Azure Portal → App Service → Get publish profile

